WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.480
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.020
In early 2019, a learning-based technique
appeared that could perform common natural

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:16.420
language processing operations, for instance,
answering questions, completing text, reading

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.910
comprehension, summarization, and more.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:25.800
This method was developed by scientists at
OpenAI, and they called it GPT-2.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.070
The key idea for GPT-2 was that all of these
problems could be formulated as different

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:36.559
variants of text completion problems, where
all we need to do is provide it an incomplete

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:40.410
piece of text, and it would try to finish
it.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:47.629
Then in June 2020 came GPT-3 that supercharged
this idea, and among many incredible examples,

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:51.870
it could generate website layouts from a written
description.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:58.190
However, no one said that these neural networks
can only deal with text information.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:04.420
And sure enough, a few months later, scientists
at OpenAI thought that if we can complete

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:09.100
text sentences, why not try to complete images
too?

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:14.659
They called this project image GPT and the
problem statement was simple: we give it an

00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:19.939
incomplete image, and we ask the AI to fill
in the missing pixels.

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:25.570
It could identify that the cat here likely
holds a piece of paper and finish the picture

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:32.359
accordingly, and even understood that if we
have a droplet here and we see just a portion

00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:37.020
of the ripples, then this means a splash must
be filled in.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:44.649
And now, right in January 2021, just 7 months
after the release of GPT-3, here is their

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:50.299
new mind-blowing technique that explores the
connection between text and images.

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:56.679
But finishing images already kind of works,
so what new thing can it do?

00:01:56.679 --> 00:02:01.100
In just a few moments, you will see that the
more appropriate question would be “what

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.950
can’t it do”?

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:07.860
For now, well, it creates images from our
written text captions, and you will see in

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.350
a moment how monumental of a challenge that
is.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:16.600
The name of this technique is a mix of Salvador
Dalí and Pixar’s Wall-e.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.040
So please meet Dall-e.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.980
And now, let’s see it through an example.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.580
For neural network-based learning methods,
it is easy to recognize that this text says

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.650
OpenAI, and what a storefront it.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.120
Images of both of these exist in abundance.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.340
Understanding that is simple.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:41.080
However, generating a storefront that says
OpenAI is quite a challenge.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.280
It is really possible that it can do that?

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.630
Well, let’s try it.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:48.620
Look, it works!

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.620
Wow!

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:56.250
Now, of course, if you look here, you immediately
see that it is by no means perfect, but let’s

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:01.930
marvel at the fact that we can get all kinds
of 2D and 3D texts, look at the storefronts

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:07.530
from different orientations, and it can deal
with all of these cases reasonably well.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:18.360
And of course, it is not limited to storefronts,
we can request license plates, bags of chips,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.120
neon signs, and more.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.700
It can really do all that.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.180
So, what else?

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.540
Well, get this, it can also kind of invent
new things.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:34.010
So let’s put our entrepreneurial hat on
and try to invent something here.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.260
For instance, let’s try to create a triangular
clock.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:39.410
Or pentagonal.

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:43.060
Or, you know, just make it a hexagon.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:48.030
It really doesn’t matter because we can
ask for absolutely anything and get a bunch

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:50.700
of prototypes in a matter of seconds.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.680
Now, let’s make it white, and…look!

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.880
Now we have a happy, happy Károly.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:03:58.880
Why is that?

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.960
It is because I am a light transport researcher
by trade, so the first thing when I look at

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:08.590
when seeing these generated images is how
physically plausible they are.

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:12.350
For instance, look at this white clock here
on the blue table.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:17.340
And it did not only put it on the table, but
it also made sure to generate appropriate

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.540
glossy reflections that matches the color
of the clock.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.020
It can do this too!

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:24.430
Loving it.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.760
Apparently, it understands geometry, shapes
and materials.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.590
I wonder what else does it understand?

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:38.530
Well, get this, for instance, it even understands
styles and rendering techniques.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:43.600
Being a graphics person, I am so happy to
see that it learned the concept of low polygon

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:54.690
count rendering, isometric views, clay objects,
and we can even add an X-ray view to the Owl.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:55.720
Kind of.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:01.040
And now, if all that wasn’t enough, hold
on to your papers, because we can also commission

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:07.570
artistic illustrations for free, and not only
that, but even have fine-grained control over

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.030
these artistic illustrations.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:15.650
I also learned that if manatees wore suits,
they would wear them like this, and after

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:21.990
a long and strenuous day walking their dogs,
they can go for yet another round… in pajamas.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:28.021
But it does not stop there, it can not only
generate paintings of nearly anything, but

00:05:28.021 --> 00:05:33.330
we can even choose the artistic style and
the time of day as well.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:37.680
The night images are a little on the nose
as most of them have the moon in the background,

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.860
but I’ll be more than happy to take these.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.300
And the best part is that you can try this
yourself right now through the link in the

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.780
video description.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:52.210
In general, not all results are perfect, but
it’s hard to even fathom all the things

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:56.840
this will enable us to do in the near future
when we can get our hands on these pre-trained

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:57.870
models.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:01.770
This may be the first technique where the
results are not limited by the algorithm,

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:04.590
but, by our own imagination.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:10.139
Now this is a quote that I said about GPT-3,
and notice that the exact same thing can be

00:06:10.139 --> 00:06:11.350
said about Dall-e.

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:17.460
Quote: “The main point is that working with
GPT-3 is a really peculiar process where we

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:23.800
know that a vast body of knowledge lies within,
but it only emerges if we can bring it out

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.860
with properly written prompts.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:31.060
It almost feels like a new kind of programming
that is open to everyone, even people without

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.570
any programming or technical knowledge.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:40.060
If a computer is a bicycle for the mind, then
GPT-3 is a fighter jet.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:41.060
Absolutely incredible."

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:46.650
I think this kind of programming is going
to be more and more common in the future.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:52.600
Now note that these are some amazing preliminary
results, but the full paper is not available

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:53.600
yet.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.620
So this was not two minutes, and it was not
about a paper.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:59.530
Welcome to Two Minute Papers!

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:04.270
Jokes aside, I cannot wait for the paper to
appear, and I’ll be here to have a closer

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:06.300
look whenever it happens.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:10.940
Make sure to subscribe and hit the bell icon
to not miss it when the big day comes.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:08:04.850
And until then, let me know in the comments
what crazy concoctions you came up with!

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:08.110
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

